# schoolOS schedule — OPS-6 (backups that nothing ran) and OPS-8 (five workers never scheduled).
#
# Install with:   crontab ops/cron/schoolos.cron
# Verify with:    node server/scripts/health-probe.js
#
# The register, verbatim:
#   OPS-6  "Backups are a script nothing runs: no cron/systemd/compose service ships, while the README
#           claims 'RPO <= 24h' — true only if an operator wires it by hand."
#   OPS-8  "Five shipped workers are never scheduled (day-worker, webhook-worker, workflow-worker,
#           dunning-reminders, reconcile-payments): outbound webhooks, workflow rules, fee dunning and
#           payment reconciliation silently never run in a real deployment."
#
# This file is that missing schedule, as a COMMITTED artifact rather than a comment in a README. It
# replaces the two commented cron variants that used to live in docker-compose.yml and README.md — those
# were not equivalent (the docker-only one wrote a flat dump with no globals, no verification and no
# retention), which is exactly how the unusable dumps in backups/manual/ came to exist.
#
# ── Why each job lives where it does ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Long-lived POLLERS are compose services, not cron lines, because a cron line that starts a poller
# every minute would start a new process every minute: outbox-worker, comms-dispatcher, webhook-worker
# and workflow-worker are all in server/docker-compose.yml.
# The THREE ONE-SHOTS below are genuinely periodic work, so they belong here.
#
# ── Exit-code contract (this is the alert transport; there is no pager integration to pretend about) ──
# Several of these scripts exit 1 to mean "I did my job and you should look":
#   dunning-reminders.js  exit 1 when reminders were queued (so an alert can fire)
#   reconcile-payments.js exit 1 when it found a discrepancy
#   retention-worker.js   exit 1 when a purge query failed
#   health-probe.js       exit 1 when a rule FIRES, 2 when the monitor itself could not run
# A bare `cron` mails any non-empty output to the operator's MTA, which is what makes these useful: the
# mail IS the alert. Do not add `|| true` to any line below — that silences the only transport there is.
# For dunning/reconcile the non-zero exit is a SUCCESS signal, so they log to a file instead of relying
# on cron mail for a "look at this" that arrives every single day.

# Adjust to the real checkout. Everything below is relative to it.
SCHOOLOS_HOME=/srv/schoolos
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin
# Postgres client tools for backup.sh; the owner DSN for the scripts that need one.
PGHOST=127.0.0.1
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=schoolos
PGDATABASE=schoolos
# OPSX-15: libpq needs a credential. Do NOT put a password in this file (cron files are typically
# world-readable) — use a root-0600 `~/.pgpass` for the operator, or an EnvironmentFile the operator
# sources. `PGPASSWORD` is deliberately not hard-coded here.
#
# OPSX-15: the redirected workers below write to /var/log/schoolos; if that directory does not exist every
# line fails at the shell. The dir is created inline in each redirected line (idempotent) rather than by a
# separate cron line, because `ops-deploy.test.js` requires every scheduled line to invoke a real worker.

# ── 02:17 IST nightly backup (OPS-6) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Certifies: globals.sql (CREATE ROLE for schoolos_app) + a custom-format db dump + `pg_restore --list`
# verification, retained 7 nights, and a `backup_last_success_epoch` row so health-probe.js can alert on
# a backup that did not happen. Without the row, "no backup in 26 hours" is unanswerable.
17 2 * * * cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && ./scripts/backup.sh

# ── 02:45 IST nightly: schema + health probe (OPS-3) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runs AFTER the backup so the probe's own failure cannot prevent the night's dump, and early enough
# that a stale API heartbeat or a stalled relay is on the operator's screen before the school day.
# NODE_ENV=production (OPSX-6): the probe defaults to 'development', which makes every production-scoped
# rule (notably comms_delivered_to_nobody) SKIP — so on the one host where the check matters, it was
# always skipped. This line is the production alert path, so it declares the production posture.
45 2 * * * cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && NODE_ENV=production node scripts/health-probe.js

# ── 03:05 IST weekdays: the umbrella day-worker (all steps except the principal brief) ──────────────
# scripts/day-worker.js sequences the tally export, the retention pass, the dunning sweep
# and the admissions follow-up SLA sweep. Named
# steps are listed explicitly so the principal brief is EXCLUDED here and runs at 07:00 instead (a brief
# computed at 03:05 reports 3am absentees, not the school day's).
# Weekdays only: there is no school business to act on over the weekend, and running it daily would
# double-queue dunning reminders against the same dedup keys as the 03:30 job below.
# (A5: admissions-followup is idempotent per lead per day, so weekday runs never double-chase.)
5 3 * * 1-5 cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && mkdir -p /var/log/schoolos && node scripts/day-worker.js preventive tally retention dunning admissions-followup library library-expiry compliance action-sla owner-security >> /var/log/schoolos/day-worker.log 2>&1

# ── 07:00 IST weekdays: the principal's morning brief (OPP-DEL-7) ───────────────────────────────────
# The one screen ROLE-NEEDS §4 asks for, computed at the START of the school day so "today's absentees"
# and "deadlines today" are real. Runs the brief step alone; exit 1 on failure so cron mail is the alert.
0 7 * * 1-5 cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && mkdir -p /var/log/schoolos && node scripts/day-worker.js principal-brief >> /var/log/schoolos/principal-brief.log 2>&1

# ── 04:00 IST daily: money ledger integrity + hash-chain drill (FEE-OPS-4) ───────────────────────────
# The live integrity half of the DR drill: the audit chain still verifies, every fee receipt still points
# at its payment, and no allocation is orphaned. Exit 1 when anything fails, so cron mail IS the alert.
# The restore half (dump → scratch DB → same checks) is ops/backup/money-drill.sh, run by hand or after a
# backup. This line is scheduled so the money path has a daily, machine-checked heartbeat.
0 4 * * * cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && mkdir -p /var/log/schoolos && node scripts/money-dr-drill.js >> /var/log/schoolos/money-drill.log 2>&1

# ── 03:30 IST weekdays: payment reconciliation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exit 1 when it finds a discrepancy between the local ledger and the gateway. Redirected to a log ON
# PURPOSE: the exit code is a finding, and every finding mailed daily is how an operator learns to
# ignore this address. `node` is invoked explicitly because the file has no shebang.
30 3 * * 1-5 cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && mkdir -p /var/log/schoolos && node scripts/reconcile-payments.js >> /var/log/schoolos/reconcile.log 2>&1

# ── 16:30 IST weekdays: homework reminders (TODO_FIX_MAP L4 / TEACHER-PARENT-STUDENT DEL-1) ──────────
# Homework due in the next 24h (a heads-up to the class's guardians) and homework overdue in the last 7
# days with no submission (a nudge to the guardians of the students who have not turned it in).
# DELIBERATELY NOT part of day-worker: that runs at 03:05 IST, inside comms quiet hours (21:00–08:00),
# so this sweep's own quiet-hours guard would skip every night. 16:30 is after the school day and before
# quiet hours. Exit 1 when reminders were queued, so cron mail is the alert.
30 16 * * 1-5 cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && mkdir -p /var/log/schoolos && node scripts/homework-reminders.js >> /var/log/schoolos/homework.log 2>&1

# 1. (the two pollers -- webhook-worker, workflow-worker -- are compose services, see docker-compose.yml)
# 2. dunning-reminders.js is DELIBERATELY NOT scheduled here.
#    day-worker.js already runs the dunning sweep (day-worker.js calls the same sweep, with the same
#    `dunning:<obligationId>` dedup keys). Scheduling both would present two independent-looking sources
#    of the same queue: the dedup key protects the DATA, but a duplicate cron line makes the SCHEDULE
#    unreadable, and "which of these two actually runs dunning?" is not a question an operator should
#    have to answer at 03:00. Run it by hand when you need it:
#        cd $SCHOOLOS_HOME/server && node scripts/dunning-reminders.js
